{"content":"🚨 **BLOCKING (re-verified on head c14d241 — still unfixed): merged cards' state history destroys itself within ~4h of the merge**

`nextHistory`'s `terminalAt` path still appends unconditionally, and merged cards are **rebuilt every tick** while they sit in the 24h `merged_recent` window (`buildFactoryState` re-derives the card from `prevCard` each tick). Every tick appends *another* `{merged_recent, at: mergedAt}` entry:

```js
if (terminalAt) {
    h.push({ state: key, at: terminalAt });   // no dedupe — pushes every tick
    return capHistory(h);
}
```

Re-reproduced on the current head by chaining `buildFactoryState` over 31 ticks (10-min cadence):

```
tick 1 history: [pr_created, validating, merged_recent@09:30]   ← correct
after 31 ticks: 24 × {merged_recent, at 09:30}                  ← real history fully evicted
```

After `HISTORY_CAP` (24) ticks (~4h at the standard cadence) the card's entire real pipeline timeline (`pr_created → review → approved_queue → validating`) is sliced out of the snapshot. The drawer renders 24 identical "Merged" rows with `0m` durations — the ticket's headline deliverable (per-state timings for bottleneck analysis) is gone exactly for the cards where it matters most. The shipped test only covers the *first* merge transition, so it cannot see this; the requested tick-over-tick regression test is still absent.

**Fix** (one guard — skip the append when the carried history already ends in that state):

```js
if (terminalAt) {
    if (!h.length || h[h.length - 1].state !== key) {
        h.push({ state: key, at: terminalAt });
    }
    return capHistory(h);
}
```

plus a regression test that builds the merged state tick-over-tick and asserts the history length stays at 3 with no duplicate terminal entries.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md"}

{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT (re-verified on head c14d241 — still unfixed): development-lane issue cards never open the details drawer**

The render pass still indexes only PR cards from the pipeline lanes:

```js
list.forEach(function (c) {
    if (c && c.pr != null) boardIndex['pr-' + c.pr + '@' + l.id] =
      { item: c, repo: st_repo, laneId: l.id };
});
```

A card in the **development lane** is an issue card, so it gets `data-key="issue-402@development"` — a key never added to `boardIndex` (only the backlog columns are indexed below). `onCardClick` → `drawerCtxFor` returns `null` → `preventDefault` is skipped → the click falls through and **navigates to GitHub** instead of opening the drawer. Meanwhile the same issue's backlog twin (`issue-402@in_dev`) opens the drawer fine. Two twins, two different behaviors — and the "In dev" card, the one an operator watches most, is the one that silently loses the feature.

Ticket AC says "details drawer works for PRs and issues"; for in-dev issues it only half-works.

**Fix:** index issue cards in the lanes loop too (`boardIndex['issue-' + c.issue + '@' + l.id] = …`), or resolve `issue-N@development` to the backlog twin's context in `drawerCtxFor`.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (re-verified — still unfixed): the backlog silently truncates at the search API's first page and is unbounded**

The v3 query fetches ALL open issues (`repo:X is:issue is:open`) in one call. GitHub search returns at most one page per call (30 by default, 100 with `per_page`), and `github_search_issues` here passes no paging — this repo's own code documents it (`js/common/smProvider.js:155`: "returns only page 1 (GitHub default 30; the tool has no perPage)"). On a repo with more open issues than one page, the `inbox` bucket is **silently truncated** while `backlogCounts` presents the partial view as authoritative ("N total"). Snapshots also grow without a bound as the issue count grows — republished every 10 minutes and rendered in full.

Practical impact today is low (the active repos have modest open-issue counts), but the snapshot's contract should be honest about completeness. Options:

- pass an explicit sort (e.g. `sort:created&order:desc`, newest N) and document the cap next to `backlogCounts`;
- or cap the inbox bucket in `buildFactoryState` (e.g. 50 newest) so the JSON size stays bounded regardless of repo;
- at minimum, note the page limit in the README's backlog section so a truncated lane isn't mistaken for "empty".

The change from the label-filtered query to all-issues is also a behavior change worth a line in the PR description's verify notes: on busy repos the search now matches far more items per tick (cost is fine — still one call — but the payload is not).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (re-verified — still unfixed): backlog cards render no age, even though the data is right there**

`cardHtml` derives the age chip from `LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]`, which only has keys for the six pipeline lanes. For backlog columns (`in_dev`/`queued`/`blocked`/`inbox`) the lookup is `undefined` → no age chip ever renders on issue cards — yet the snapshot *does* carry the per-card time: `devStartedAt` for in-dev cards and the last `history` entry's `at` (stamped on every bucket transition) for the rest.

Suggested: when `LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]` misses, fall back to the newest history entry:

```js
var enteredAt = c[LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]];
if (!enteredAt && c.history && c.history.length) {
    var last = c.history[c.history.length - 1];
    enteredAt = last.at || null;   // first-ever entry may have no `at` — honest unknown
}
```

That gives every backlog card a "how long has it been sitting here" chip — arguably the most useful number in the new lanes (age of the inbox, age of the block).
