A card in the **development lane** is an issue card, so it gets `data-key="issue-402@development"` — a key never added to `boardIndex` (only the backlog columns are indexed below). `onCardClick` → `drawerCtxFor` returns `null` → `preventDefault` is skipped → the click falls through and **navigates to GitHub** instead of opening the drawer. Meanwhile the same issue's backlog twin (`issue-402@in_dev`) opens the drawer fine. Two twins, two different behaviors — and the "In dev" card, the one an operator watches most, is the one that silently loses the feature.

Ticket AC says "details drawer works for PRs and issues"; for in-dev issues it only half-works.

**Fix:** index issue cards in the lanes loop too (`boardIndex['issue-' + c.issue + '@' + l.id] = …`), or resolve `issue-N@development` to the backlog twin's context in `drawerCtxFor`.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (3rd verification — still unfixed): the backlog silently truncates at the search API's first page and is unbounded**

The v3 query fetches ALL open issues (`repo:X is:issue is:open`) in one call. GitHub search returns at most one page per call (30 by default, 100 with `per_page`), and `github_search_issues` here passes no paging — this repo's own code documents it (`js/common/smProvider.js:155`: "returns only page 1 (GitHub default 30; the tool has no perPage)"). On a repo with more open issues than one page, the `inbox` bucket is **silently truncated** while `backlogCounts` presents the partial view as authoritative ("N total"). Snapshots also grow without a bound as the issue count grows — republished every 10 minutes and rendered in full.

Practical impact today is low (the active repos have modest open-issue counts), but the snapshot's contract should be honest about completeness. Options:

- pass an explicit sort (e.g. `sort:created&order:desc`, newest N) and document the cap next to `backlogCounts`;
- or cap the inbox bucket in `buildFactoryState` (e.g. 50 newest) so the JSON size stays bounded regardless of repo;
- at minimum, note the page limit in the README's backlog section so a truncated lane isn't mistaken for "empty".

The change from the label-filtered query to all-issues is also a behavior change worth a line in the PR description's verify notes: on busy repos the search now matches far more items per tick (cost is fine — still one call — but the payload is not).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (3rd verification — still unfixed): backlog cards render no age, even though the data is right there**

`cardHtml` derives the age chip from `LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]`, which only has keys for the six pipeline lanes. For backlog columns (`in_dev`/`queued`/`blocked`/`inbox`) the lookup is `undefined` → no age chip ever renders on issue cards — yet the snapshot *does* carry the per-card time: `devStartedAt` for in-dev cards and the last `history` entry's `at` (stamped on every bucket transition) for the rest.

Suggested: when `LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]` misses, fall back to the newest history entry:

```js
var enteredAt = c[LANE_ENTERED_AT[laneId]];
if (!enteredAt && c.history && c.history.length) {
    var last = c.history[c.history.length - 1];
    enteredAt = last.at || null;   // first-ever entry may have no `at` — honest unknown
}
```

That gives every backlog card a "how long has it been sitting here" chip — arguably the most useful number in the new lanes (age of the inbox, age of the block).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment7.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (3rd verification — still unfixed): badge HTML is duplicated between `cardHtml` and `drawerHeaderHtml`**

These two blocks (here and at line 376) are byte-identical (hot-label highlighting + the `blocked` class):

```js
var badges = (c.labels || []).map(function (l) {
  return '<span class="lbl' + (hot.indexOf(l) >= 0 ? ' hot' : '') +
         (l === 'blocked' ? ' lbl-blocked' : '') + '">' + esc(l) + '</span>';
}).join('');
```

The card and the drawer must stay visually consistent by construction, not by copy-paste — the next label-styling tweak (e.g. adding `agent:dev` coloring) will land in one and miss the other. Extract once near `cardHtml`:

```js
function badgesHtml(c) {
  var hot = CFG.badgeLabels || [];
  return (c.labels || []).map(function (l) {
    return '<span class="lbl' + (hot.indexOf(l) >= 0 ? ' hot' : '') +
      (l === 'blocked' ? ' lbl-blocked' : '') + '">' + esc(l) + '</span>';
  }).join('');
}
```

and use it in both places (the href-building block is similarly near-duplicated and could share a small helper, but the badges are the clear win).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment8.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (3rd verification — still unfixed): status pill reports "issues: 0 …" for pre-v3 snapshots**

`st.backlogCounts` is undefined on snapshots published before this change, so `bc[c.id] || 0` zeroes every bucket and the pill always renders `issues: 0 in-dev · 0 queued · 0 blocked · 0 inbox` — a false "backlog is empty" signal during any window where an old-SM snapshot is still live on a `factory-data` branch (upgrade skew), and today on schema-1 history files.

Cheap fix — only claim what the snapshot declares:

```js
if (st.backlog) {
  document.getElementById('backlog-pill').textContent = 'issues: ' + backlogSummary;
} else {
  document.getElementById('backlog-pill').hidden = true;   // pre-v3 snapshot
}
```

(remember to un-hide it on the next render). Same spirit as the drawer's `"—"` fallback: absent data renders as absent, not as zeros.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment9.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION (3rd verification — still unfixed): the hardcoded `DEFAULT_MACHINE_AUTHOR` overrides "unconfigured", not just "unset"**

```js
var machineAuthor = input.machineAuthor || DEFAULT_MACHINE_AUTHOR;
```

`resolveMachineAuthor` (js/common/machineAuthor.js) deliberately returns **null** when a deployment has configured no `machineAuthor` — its doc contract says "3. `null` — no machine author configured … fail-closed", and "this repo never hardcodes it". This `||` collapses that meaningful state into the hardcoded `'ai-teammate'` login. For a factory that never set the knob, any issue assigned to a user literally named `ai-teammate` (or to whatever that fallback string matches in their org) would land in the "In dev · assigned" lane without the deployment ever opting in. The header comment calls it a "deployment knob" — a knob should have no opinion when unset.

Safer contract: treat null as "no assignment bucketing" and keep the constant only for tests/back-compat:

```js
var machineAuthor = input.machineAuthor || null;
...
var assigned = !!machineAuthor && (/* assignees/assignee checks */);
```

Low practical risk (the login is machine-flavored), but it's the kind of silent default that surprises the next factory adopting the board — and it now directly contradicts the helper this PR feeds from (`smAgent.js` passes `resolveMachineAuthor(...)`, whose null the builder throws away).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment10.md"}

